IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `spam-act`

-- Spam Control Act 2007 (Singapore) -- Part 4, the civil action (ss 12 to 15).
--
-- This is the part that matters to a person who has been spammed. The Act gives
-- no regulator a power to fine and creates no offence; everything turns on
-- s 13, which lets a person who has suffered loss sue, and on s 14, which says
-- what they can get.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules
-- skill. No pipeline, no independent test pass, no human gate.

§ `Spam Control Act 2007 -- Part 4, civil action`

§§ `12 -- aiding, abetting, and the carriers' safe harbour`

-- s 12(1): a person must not --
--   (a) "aid, abet or procure" a contravention of s 9 or s 11;
--   (b) "induce, whether by threats, promises or otherwise," such a
--       contravention;
--   (c) "be in any way, directly or indirectly, KNOWINGLY concerned in or a
--       party to," such a contravention; or
--   (d) "conspire with others to effect" such a contravention.
--
-- Only limb (c) carries a mental element on its face. Limb (a) does not, which
-- is a departure from the general law of abetment, where the abettor must know
-- the circumstances.
DECLARE SecondaryConduct HAS
    `aided, abetted or procured a contravention of section 9 or 11` IS A BOOLEAN
    `induced a contravention by threats, promises or otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
    `was knowingly concerned in or a party to a contravention` IS A BOOLEAN
    `conspired with others to effect a contravention` IS A BOOLEAN
    `provides or operates facilities for online services or network access` IS A BOOLEAN
    `provides services relating to or connections for the transmission or routing of data` IS A BOOLEAN

-- s 12(2): a person "does not contravene subsection (1), SECTION 9 OR 11 merely
-- because the person -- (a) provides, or operates facilities for, online
-- services or network access; or (b) provides services relating to, or provides
-- connections for, the transmission or routing of data."
--
-- The safe harbour reaches all three prohibitions, not just s 12(1). It is
-- qualified by "merely because": it protects the carrier for carrying, and
-- nothing more. A provider who also harvests addresses, or who sends on its own
-- account, is outside it.
GIVEN `the conduct` IS A SecondaryConduct
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is within the section 12(2) safe harbour` IF
        `the conduct`'s `provides or operates facilities for online services or network access`
    OR  `the conduct`'s `provides services relating to or connections for the transmission or routing of data`

GIVEN `the conduct` IS A SecondaryConduct
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a limb of section 12(1) is made out` IF
        `the conduct`'s `aided, abetted or procured a contravention of section 9 or 11`
    OR  `the conduct`'s `induced a contravention by threats, promises or otherwise`
    OR  `the conduct`'s `was knowingly concerned in or a party to a contravention`
    OR  `the conduct`'s `conspired with others to effect a contravention`

-- Read together: the safe harbour answers the whole question only where the
-- carrier's conduct IS the provision of the service. Where a limb of s 12(1) is
-- independently made out -- say the provider conspired -- the words "merely
-- because" mean s 12(2) does not save it. This encoding treats a made-out limb
-- as taking the person outside "merely because", which is the reading the words
-- carry; it is an interpretive choice and is recorded as one.
GIVEN `the conduct` IS A SecondaryConduct
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person contravenes section 12(1)` IF
    `a limb of section 12(1) is made out` `the conduct`

§§ `13 -- who may sue, and whom`

-- s 13(1): "Where there is a contravention of section 9 or 11 in relation to
-- electronic messages sent to electronic addresses, ANY PERSON WHO HAS SUFFERED
-- LOSS OR DAMAGE as a direct or an indirect result of that contravention may
-- commence an action in a court against -- (a) the sender; or (b) a person who
-- has contravened section 12(1)."
--
-- Three things to notice.
--
-- First, the gateway is a contravention of s 9 or s 11 and nothing else. A
-- contravention of s 12(1) alone will not do: s 12(1) is itself defined by
-- reference to a contravention of s 9 or s 11, so there must be a primary
-- contravention before anyone can be sued for helping.
--
-- Second, loss or damage is the standing requirement. Receiving spam is not
-- itself loss. s 14(5) says the loss "includes any pecuniary loss", which widens
-- what counts but leaves the requirement in place. A recipient annoyed by a
-- hundred messages and out of pocket by nothing has no action.
--
-- Third, the words "any person" are not confined to recipients. An Internet
-- access service provider whose network carried the traffic, or a business whose
-- domain was forged in the header information, suffers loss as a direct or
-- indirect result and is within the section.
DECLARE Claim HAS
    `there is a contravention of section 9 or 11` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the claimant has suffered loss or damage as a direct or indirect result` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the defendant is the sender` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the defendant has contravened section 12(1)` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an action under section 13(1) lies` IF
        `the claim`'s `there is a contravention of section 9 or 11`
    AND `the claim`'s `the claimant has suffered loss or damage as a direct or indirect result`
    AND (    `the claim`'s `the defendant is the sender`
         OR  `the claim`'s `the defendant has contravened section 12(1)`)

-- s 13(2): "This section does not affect any liability that any person has under
-- any other written law or rule of law." So the absence of an action under this
-- Act decides nothing about trespass, passing off, the Personal Data Protection
-- Act 2012, or anything else.
GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `this Act is the last word on the defendant's liability` IF
    FALSE

§§ `14 -- injunction, damages, and the election`

-- s 14(1): "the types of relief that the court MAY grant INCLUDE the following:
--   (a) an injunction (subject to any terms that the court thinks fit);
--   (b) damages; (c) statutory damages under subsection (3)."
-- s 14(2): "(b) and (c) ... are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE."
-- s 14(3): "the claimant is ENTITLED, AT THE ELECTION OF THE CLAIMANT, to --
--   (a) damages in the amount of the loss or damage suffered ...; or
--   (b) statutory damages -- (i) not exceeding $25 for each electronic message
--       mentioned in section 13(1); and (ii) not exceeding in the aggregate
--       $1 million, UNLESS the claimant proves that the actual loss suffered by
--       the claimant from such electronic messages EXCEEDS $1 million."
--
-- s 14(1) says the court "may" grant and that the list "includes"; s 14(3) says
-- the claimant "is entitled" and elects. The two are in tension over who
-- decides. This encoding follows s 14(3), the more specific provision, and
-- computes what the claimant may elect; it does not model a residual discretion
-- to refuse what s 14(3) grants.
--
-- The injunction in s 14(1)(a) is outside the mutual exclusion, so it is
-- available alongside whichever of (b) and (c) is elected.

DECLARE Award HAS
    `the number of electronic messages mentioned in section 13(1)` IS A NUMBER
    `the actual loss suffered by the claimant, in dollars` IS A NUMBER

`the per-message maximum in section 14(3)(b)(i)` MEANS 25
`the aggregate cap in section 14(3)(b)(ii)` MEANS 1000000

-- s 14(3)(b)(i): $25 for each message.
GIVEN `the award` IS AN Award
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the per-message ceiling` IS
    `the award`'s `the number of electronic messages mentioned in section 13(1)`
        TIMES `the per-message maximum in section 14(3)(b)(i)`

-- s 14(3)(b)(ii): the aggregate cap, and the condition on which it lifts. Note
-- that the condition is "EXCEEDS $1 million" -- a loss of exactly $1 million
-- does not exceed it and the cap stands.
GIVEN `the award` IS AN Award
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the aggregate cap is lifted` IF
    `the award`'s `the actual loss suffered by the claimant, in dollars`
        GREATER THAN `the aggregate cap in section 14(3)(b)(ii)`

-- Both limbs of s 14(3)(b) bind at once, so the ceiling is the lower of them,
-- except where the aggregate limb has been lifted and only the per-message limb
-- remains.
GIVEN `the award` IS AN Award
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the most the claimant may have as statutory damages` IS
    IF   `the aggregate cap is lifted` `the award`
    THEN `the per-message ceiling` `the award`
    ELSE IF   `the per-message ceiling` `the award`
                  LESS THAN `the aggregate cap in section 14(3)(b)(ii)`
         THEN `the per-message ceiling` `the award`
         ELSE `the aggregate cap in section 14(3)(b)(ii)`

-- s 14(3)(a): the other election, measured by the loss itself.
GIVEN `the award` IS AN Award
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the most the claimant may have as damages` IS
    `the award`'s `the actual loss suffered by the claimant, in dollars`

-- The election a claimant acting in their own interest would make, and what it
-- is worth. s 14(2) forbids both.
GIVEN `the award` IS AN Award
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the most the claimant may recover on the better election` IS
    IF   `the most the claimant may have as statutory damages` `the award`
             GREATER THAN `the most the claimant may have as damages` `the award`
    THEN `the most the claimant may have as statutory damages` `the award`
    ELSE `the most the claimant may have as damages` `the award`

GIVEN `the award` IS AN Award
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `statutory damages are the better election` IF
    `the most the claimant may have as statutory damages` `the award`
        GREATER THAN `the most the claimant may have as damages` `the award`

-- The escape from the aggregate cap is narrower than it looks, and the two
-- functions below are here to make that visible rather than to answer a question
-- anyone asks.
--
-- To get statutory damages above $1 million, the claimant must prove an actual
-- loss above $1 million. But proving an actual loss above $1 million also makes
-- the s 14(3)(a) election worth that loss. So the lifted cap is worth having
-- only where $25 a message comes to more than the loss proved -- which needs
-- more than 40,000 messages before the cap is in play at all, and then needs the
-- loss to be small relative to the message count.
GIVEN `the award` IS AN Award
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `lifting the aggregate cap is worth anything to this claimant` IF
        `the aggregate cap is lifted` `the award`
    AND `the per-message ceiling` `the award`
            GREATER THAN `the aggregate cap in section 14(3)(b)(ii)`
    AND `the per-message ceiling` `the award`
            GREATER THAN `the award`'s `the actual loss suffered by the claimant, in dollars`

-- And the discontinuity. At a loss of exactly $1 million the cap holds; at
-- $1 million and one dollar it is gone, and the ceiling jumps to $25 a message.
-- For a claimant with enough messages the jump is worth more than the dollar
-- that caused it by six orders of magnitude. The cases file asserts the pair.
GIVEN `the award` IS AN Award
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `what the ceiling would be if the loss were one dollar higher` IS
    `the most the claimant may have as statutory damages`
        (Award WITH
            `the number of electronic messages mentioned in section 13(1)` IS `the award`'s `the number of electronic messages mentioned in section 13(1)`
            `the actual loss suffered by the claimant, in dollars` IS `the award`'s `the actual loss suffered by the claimant, in dollars` PLUS 1)

GIVEN `the award` IS AN Award
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `what one more dollar of proved loss adds to the ceiling` IS
    `what the ceiling would be if the loss were one dollar higher` `the award`
        MINUS `the most the claimant may have as statutory damages` `the award`

§§ `14(4) -- what the court weighs in fixing statutory damages`

-- s 14(4): in awarding statutory damages the court "is to have regard to --
--   (a) whether the contravention by the defendant of section 9, 11 or 12(1) was
--       WILFUL;
--   (b) any loss or damage that the claimant has suffered or is likely to
--       suffer ...;
--   (c) any benefit shown to have accrued to the defendant by reason of the
--       sending of electronic messages;
--   (d) the need to deter other similar instances of sending ...; and
--   (e) all other relevant matters."
--
-- This decides nothing: it is a direction to the court, and limb (e) is open. It
-- is encoded as a record so that the factors are named and can be carried, and
-- the function below reports only how many of the four specific ones are
-- present. It does not compute an award, and nothing in the Act lets it.
--
-- Limb (a) is worth stopping on. Neither s 9 nor s 11 contains any mental
-- element -- both are flat prohibitions -- so wilfulness is irrelevant to
-- whether the Act was contravened and relevant only to what the contravention
-- costs. A sender who harvests addresses deliberately and one who inherits a
-- bought list in good faith contravene s 9 alike, and are distinguished at the
-- remedy stage or not at all.
DECLARE StatutoryDamagesFactors HAS
    `the contravention was wilful` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the claimant has suffered or is likely to suffer loss or damage` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a benefit is shown to have accrued to the defendant` IS A BOOLEAN
    `there is a need to deter other similar instances` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the factors` IS A StatutoryDamagesFactors
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `how many of the four specific factors in section 14(4) are present` IS
    (IF `the factors`'s `the contravention was wilful` THEN 1 ELSE 0)
        PLUS (IF `the factors`'s `the claimant has suffered or is likely to suffer loss or damage` THEN 1 ELSE 0)
        PLUS (IF `the factors`'s `a benefit is shown to have accrued to the defendant` THEN 1 ELSE 0)
        PLUS (IF `the factors`'s `there is a need to deter other similar instances` THEN 1 ELSE 0)

-- Wilfulness is a factor and not an element. Stated as its own function because
-- it is the kind of thing a reader assumes the other way round.
GIVEN `the factors` IS A StatutoryDamagesFactors
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `wilfulness is required for the contravention` IF
    FALSE

§§ `15 -- costs and expenses`

-- s 15: "In any proceedings under this Act, the court may, IN ADDITION to
-- exercising the powers conferred by section 14, order the defendant to pay to
-- the claimant -- (a) the costs and expenses of and incidental to the
-- proceedings; and (b) any legal costs incurred in respect of the proceedings."
--
-- "In addition to" puts this outside the s 14(2) mutual exclusion, so costs run
-- whichever election was made. The section names only the defendant as payer:
-- it confers no power to order a claimant to pay, which leaves an unsuccessful
-- claimant's costs to the court's general jurisdiction and not to this Act.
GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court may order the defendant to pay the claimant's costs under section 15` IF
    `an action under section 13(1) lies` `the claim`

GIVEN `the claim` IS A Claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 15 lets the court order the claimant to pay the defendant's costs` IF
    FALSE
